- id: D261017-6x46f
  timestamp: 2026-10-16T13:16:07Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-17T05:51:42Z
  summary: Clarify symbol-link parsing and correct Link's code-name metadata.
  rationale: Symbol fingerprint modes require a clean parse of the whole file; byte fingerprints do not parse source syntax. Replace the misleading JSON-as-invalid-Python example with an actual syntax-error case and a byte-mode boundary. Reference is a conceptual distinction, not an existing code name, so keep it in prose rather than the not list.
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - LINKS-001
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Link
- id: D261017-q54ty
  timestamp: 2026-10-17T00:30:57Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-17T00:39:26Z
  summary: Add manual checks for rule meaning, agent proposals and human approval, and strengthen the corresponding mechanical tests.
  rationale: Automated tests can check case metadata and CLI lifecycle transitions, but cannot judge whether a requirement is meaningful, an agent presents work honestly, or a person explicitly approved a specific decision. Keep partial mechanical tests unlinked until mixed automated and manual verification is supported; track that limitation as a high priority Beans bug. Preserve the existing rule statements and fully automated coverage for CORE-002 and LINKS-001.
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-001
  - CORE-004
  - CORE-005
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261017-4zy7n
  timestamp: 2026-10-17T05:28:33Z
  actor: agent
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-17T05:51:42Z
  summary: 'Record the history and stale-link guarantees that let Statute keep rules true: active decisions are immutable, agreed rules and terms change only with a decision, changed targets make links stale, and only a review verdict refreshes them.'
  rationale: These are the checks behind the one-pager's promise that nobody quietly edits a rule to match the code and that a change to linked code or tests brings the rule up for review. Each rule gets a dedicated contract test so its test link changes only when its own cases change. Base, Stale link and Missing link are the vocabulary these rules and the CLI output use.
  establishes:
  - HIST-001
  - HIST-002
  - HIST-003
  - LINKS-002
  - LINKS-003
  changes: []
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Base
  - Stale link
  - Missing link
- id: D261017-rlpdd
  timestamp: 2026-10-17T06:06:51Z
  actor: agent
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-17T21:24:06Z
  summary: 'Record the missing-link, broken-rule and decision-lifecycle guarantees: a gone or ambiguous target is missing until relinked, a built rule with an open gap is broken, a rule or term is in at most one proposed decision, and ratify activates exactly the named decisions or writes nothing.'
  rationale: 'These checks keep a broken or unapproved state from being cleared quietly: review cannot clear a missing link, broken follows the gaps file, overlapping proposals cannot be approved by accident, and ratify never agrees more than the person approved or leaves an invalid result. Each area has a dedicated contract test. Fingerprint, Draft rule and Proposed decision are the vocabulary the CLI and these rules use.'
  establishes:
  - LINKS-004
  - GAPS-001
  - DEC-001
  - DEC-002
  - DEC-003
  changes: []
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Fingerprint
  - Draft rule
  - Proposed decision
- id: D261018-avopb
  timestamp: 2026-10-18T20:20:31Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-18T20:32:24Z
  summary: Everything in an agreed term's section except its `yaml` block is its definition, changes only with a decision, and is shown next to the rules that use the term. Link's paragraph after its `yaml` block, present when D261017-6x46f approved Link, now counts as part of Link's definition.
  rationale: 'A paragraph after the first can qualify a term''s meaning, as Link''s does, but the history check compared only the first paragraph and the rule and file views of `statute show` printed only that paragraph. A missed meaning change is worse than a false alarm (AGENTS.md, principle 1), and the human glossary needs one clear approved text to stay consistent with. `aliases`, `avoid`, `agreed` and comments in the `yaml` block stay uncompared. Rejected: treating later paragraphs as notes that are not compared, as rule notes are; a note that qualifies the meaning would then be caught only by review.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - HIST-003
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261018-d0ld8
  timestamp: 2026-10-18T20:29:04Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-18T20:32:24Z
  summary: 'A rule retired in the base never changes: it keeps its section, its compared fields and `agreed: retired` whatever decision lists it, and `decide` refuses to list it in `changes` or `retires`. DEC-001''s boundary case now names agreed rules only.'
  rationale: 'Spec 4.1 says a retired rule keeps its section and its ID is never reused, and active decisions still name it, but the history check protected only agreed rules, and `decide --changes` set a retired rule back to proposed, which revived it. This is a separate rule rather than a wider HIST-002, because HIST-002 lets a decision carry a change and this rule lets none. DEC-001''s boundary said any rule established by an active decision can be listed in a new proposed decision, which is no longer true of retired rules. Rejected: reviving a retired rule through a decision, which would let one ID mean different things over time.'
  establishes:
  - HIST-004
  changes:
  - DEC-001
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261018-m21p6
  timestamp: 2026-10-18T21:01:10Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-18T21:23:20Z
  summary: 'An agreed term can be retired by a decision that lists it in `retires_terms`. It stays agreed until ratified, then reads `agreed: retired` and keeps its section as a record. Only retired rules and active decisions may then name it. `show` labels it, and name and `avoid` checks skip it (spec only).'
  rationale: 'Removing a term breaks the active decisions and retired rules that name it, and history keeps those immutable, so a term nobody uses any more had to stay agreed for ever. Keeping the retired section as a record keeps every historical reference valid. `retires_terms` is a separate field, so `retires` keeps meaning rule IDs and `terms` keeps meaning introduces or redefines. A decision cannot redefine and retire the same term: the record keeps the approved meaning, and a successor is a new term. DEC-003 changes because a rule outside the ratified decisions that still uses a retiring term now blocks `ratify`. Agreement status and Proposed decision now cover retired terms. Rejected: deleting retired terms, aliases that redirect to a successor, a machine-readable replacement field, and reviving a retired term.'
  establishes:
  - DEC-004
  - DEC-005
  changes:
  - DEC-003
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Agreement status
  - Proposed decision
- id: D261018-0yeyg
  timestamp: 2026-10-18T21:01:16Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-18T21:23:20Z
  summary: 'A term retired in the base never changes: its section, definition, `code`, `not` and `agreed: retired` stay, whatever decision lists it, and `decide` refuses to list it. Ratify with or after D261018-m21p6, which redefines Agreement status, a term HIST-005 uses.'
  rationale: 'Active decisions keep naming a retired term, so its meaning must stay what they approved, as HIST-004 keeps a retired rule. This is a separate rule rather than a wider HIST-003, because HIST-003 lets a decision carry a change and this rule lets none; a new meaning gets a new term name. It reuses HIST-004''s history check rather than adding a second mechanism. Rejected: reviving a retired term through `decide --terms`, which would let one name mean different things over time.'
  establishes:
  - HIST-005
  changes: []
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261028-qy5et
  timestamp: 2026-10-28T10:04:04Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-10-28T10:16:42Z
  summary: Rename the ratify command, skill and decision field to approve and approved_by. The rules and terms that name them change wording only.
  rationale: Approve is the word the software industry already uses for a person accepting a change, so PMs and designers need no new vocabulary. Meaning is unchanged. Keeping ratify was rejected because it is unfamiliar; a backward-compatible alias was rejected because Statute has no users yet.
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-004
  - CORE-005
  - DEC-002
  - DEC-003
  - DEC-004
  - DEC-005
  - HIST-004
  - HIST-005
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Approval
  - Proposed decision
- id: D261108-4ssd6
  timestamp: 2026-11-08T05:56:10Z
  actor: agent
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-08T06:20:18Z
  summary: List the code names that implement nine terms whose code lists were empty or missed the main type. Definitions do not change.
  rationale: 'Eight terms had code: [] and Link listed only the private LinkLine struct, leaving out model::Link. The spec example lists a name identical to its term (Session -> Session), and nothing treats a term''s own name as a code name, so empty lists left these terms unconnected to code and check --all could not catch a rename. Every added name appears as written outside Markdown and statute/. Rejected: leaving same-name terms empty by convention, because the spec does not define one; adding the PR impact Verdict to Rule review''s not list, because no qualified name for it appears in the code and check --all would warn.'
  establishes: []
  changes: []
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Term
  - Rule
  - Decision
  - Gap
  - Manual check
  - Approval
  - Rule review
  - Affected rule
  - Link
- id: D261108-53vdg
  timestamp: 2026-11-08T11:16:21Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-08T11:47:54Z
  summary: Fall back to a whole-file link when a requested Swift symbol file does not parse cleanly.
  rationale: The Swift grammar can reject valid Swift. A whole-file link is conservative and remains useful when symbol parsing fails; refusing it would leave the agent to discover and create the whole-file link manually. Missing or ambiguous symbols still fail, and existing symbol links remain missing until relinked. The fallback does not retain the requested symbol in the lock; recording intent can be considered later if needed.
  establishes:
  - LINKS-005
  changes: []
  retires: []
  terms: []
- id: D261115-ywulh
  timestamp: 2026-11-15T01:07:56Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-15T01:39:40Z
  summary: A rule is verified by test links, a manual check, or both; a manual check can be its only verification. Test links never replace or complete a manual check, so it stays required while stated. status, review and the PR "Rule impact" section show each listed rule's manual check.
  rationale: 'Some rules have behaviour a test can check and behaviour only a person can judge (bug st-8j2x). When a manual check counted only in place of a test link, linking a test hid the manual requirement, and partial tests looked like coverage of the whole rule. The completeness condition stays the same (a code link, and a test link or a manual check), so existing built rules stay built. Rejected: requiring a test link on every rule, which breaks rules no test can judge; a new field or state that records that a person did the check, which would make Statute track human consent; and treating passing tests or a Git identity as evidence that the manual check was done.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-003
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Manual check
  - Build state
- id: D261115-8tnxv
  timestamp: 2026-11-15T03:02:24Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-15T03:03:11Z
  summary: Merge Code link and Test link into Link. Link's definition now states both roles, and CORE-003 and LINKS-002 list Link instead. Code link and Test link are retired; Link is their successor.
  rationale: 'Both role terms were used by exactly CORE-003 and LINKS-002, never changed after adoption, and had one code name each, so their separate identities added no precision (review record in Bean st-uvvi). Link keeps every sentence of both roles and both LinkRole code names, so CORE-003 still explains code and test links. Cost: show Link now also returns CORE-003, and LINKS-001, LINKS-003, LINKS-004 and LINKS-005 print the role text. Rejected: keeping the roles as aliases, because aliases are not redirects; keeping both terms, because they add contention and duplicate text.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-003
  - LINKS-002
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Link
  retires_terms:
  - Code link
  - Test link
- id: D261115-hy140
  timestamp: 2026-11-15T03:02:24Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-15T03:03:11Z
  summary: Merge Affected rule into Rule review. Rule review's definition now says what an affected rule is, and lists RuleImpact. Affected rule is retired; Rule review is its successor.
  rationale: 'No rule listed Affected rule, and it always changed together with Rule review. Rule review already used the phrase, but rule views do not expand prose, so LINKS-003 and LINKS-004 used it without a definition; the merge makes Rule review self-contained (review record in Bean st-uvvi). RuleImpact names the impact result rather than the review, a small loss of precision that keeps the name checked. Rejected: keeping Affected rule, which no rule referenced.'
  establishes: []
  changes: []
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Rule review
  retires_terms:
  - Affected rule
- id: D261115-8hxqc
  timestamp: 2026-11-15T03:37:15Z
  actor: user
  status: active
  approved_by:
    name: Anton Solo
    email: anton@50lo.me
    at: 2026-11-15T03:39:43Z
  summary: Merge Proposed decision into Decision. Decision's definition now states the proposal lifecycle, and DEC-001, DEC-002, DEC-004 and DEC-005 list Decision instead. Proposed decision is retired; Decision is its successor.
  rationale: 'Changes to how proposals work are rare, so holding Decision in a proposed decision for such a change costs little under DEC-001. The merged definition keeps every condition of Proposed decision and its code name, and gives CORE-005, HIST-001, HIST-004 and HIST-005, whose text already relies on proposal semantics, the definition in their rule views (review record in Bean st-uvvi). Cost: Decision grows from 26 to 79 words in 12 rule views. Rejected: keeping both terms and adding Proposed decision to those four rules, which keeps a second term for the same lifecycle.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - DEC-001
  - DEC-002
  - DEC-004
  - DEC-005
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Decision
  retires_terms:
  - Proposed decision
- id: D261115-kj2d6
  timestamp: 2026-11-15T03:49:57Z
  actor: user
  status: proposed
  approved_by: null
  summary: Merge Protected case and Boundary case into Rule. Rule's definition now states what its protected and boundary cases are, and CORE-001 lists Rule only. Protected case and Boundary case are retired; Rule is their successor.
  rationale: 'Both case terms describe fields of a rule, were listed only by CORE-001, never changed after adoption, and had one code name each (review record in Bean st-uvvi). Rule keeps both meanings apart: a protected case is behavior to preserve, a boundary case is nearby valid behavior outside scope. Cost: Rule grows from 25 to 73 words in 10 rule views. Rejected: keeping both terms, the review record''s first recommendation; one umbrella case term, which would blur the two purposes.'
  establishes: []
  changes:
  - CORE-001
  retires: []
  terms:
  - Rule
  retires_terms:
  - Protected case
  - Boundary case
